.TH HD-PLYMOUTH 7 "October 2026" "hd-desktop" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-plymouth \- the boot splash of HyprDebian
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-desktop
holds the
.BR plymouth (8)
theme
.BR hyprdebian ,
the boot splash of HyprDebian, and makes it the theme of plymouth; see
.BR hd-desktop (7).
It looks like the lock screen of
.BR hyprlock (1),
as
.I ~/.config/hypr/hyprlock.conf
of hd-desktop sets it up, in the theme of the system of
.BR hd-theme (7):
the background of the theme from
.BR hd-art (7),
scaled to cover the screen, and, when the boot asks for a password, a rounded
input field in the middle with the colour
.B dark
at its
.B opacity
as the fill and an outline in
.BR highlight .
.PP
The script of plymouth cannot read the theme, so
.BR hd-plymouth-render (8)
renders the files of the plymouth theme from it into
.IR /var/lib/hd-plymouth/theme .
Selecting the theme of the system with
.B hd-theme \-\-select
as root renders them again and rebuilds the initramfs.
.PP
The theme is based on the theme emerald of the Debian package
.BR desktop-base .
hd-desktop conflicts with desktop-base, whose themes it replaces.
.SH PASSWORDS
The ZFS script of the initramfs asks for the password of an encrypted
dataset with plymouth when the splash shows, up to three times.
The field does not show the prompt of the question.
The empty field says
.IR "Enter Password" ;
every key press adds a dot in the colour
.BR light .
After Enter, the outline turns yellow while the password is checked, and
the field hides after about two seconds.
When the same prompt comes back, the password was wrong: the outline turns
red and the field says how many attempts failed, until the next key press.
.PP
The text is in the font of the theme, in the colour
.BR light .
The hook hd-plymouth for initramfs-tools puts its font files in the
initramfs, as the hook of plymouth only takes the default fonts.
.SH SETTING THE THEME
plymouth reads its theme from
.IR /etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf .
The package diverts the one of plymouth to
.I /etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf.plymouth
and copies its own in its place, on every installation and upgrade.
The settings that it leaves out come from
.IR /usr/share/plymouth/plymouthd.defaults .
Removing the package puts the one of plymouth back.
.PP
The initramfs holds the splash.
The hook hd-plymouth renders it with
.BR hd-plymouth-render (8)
and copies
.I /var/lib/hd-plymouth/theme
to the same path in the initramfs, with the background that
.I background.png
links to.
Installing, upgrading or removing the package has initramfs-tools rebuild
it, and hd-bootloader then rebuilds its UKIs.
The splash of a shutdown or reboot, which plymouthd shows from the root file
system, uses the same files.
.PP
The splash shows when the kernel command line has
.BR splash ,
which hd-bootloader adds by default.
The bootloader passes its resolution with
.BR video= ,
so the splash starts at that resolution.
.SH HANDING OVER TO HYPRLAND
At the end of the boot, the screen goes from the splash to Hyprland without
the console in between.
.B hd-plymouth-handoff.service
takes the place of
.BR plymouth-quit.service ,
which would show the console.
greetd wants it, and it runs before greetd starts:
.B plymouth deactivate
stops the splash, which stays on the screen.
greetd starts the session of Hyprland on tty1, the VT of the splash, without
a VT switch and without setting the VT to text mode; see
.BR hd-desktop (7).
Once Hyprland shows its first frame,
.B plymouth quit \-\-retain\-splash
leaves the screen to it.
plymouth must not quit before that: the kernel turns off the planes that
show the framebuffers of a process that exits, and the screen would be black
until Hyprland draws.
The DRM files
.I state
and
.I framebuffer
in
.I /sys/kernel/debug/dri
tell when no plane shows a framebuffer of plymouthd any more.
Without them, plymouth quits once Hyprland has the display open, which can
be a moment before its first frame.
.PP
When greetd fails, or logs nobody in and starts the console on tty1, or
when Hyprland does not show a frame after 30 seconds, plymouth quits as
usual and the console shows.
.B plymouth-quit-wait.service
returns at once when the splash is handed over, as greetd starts after it;
otherwise it waits for plymouth as usual.
.PP
Hyprland shows the colour
.B dark
of the theme until hyprpaper draws the wallpaper, with
.B disable_hyprland_logo
in the config of Hyprland of the user.
When Hyprland sets another mode than the one of the splash, the screen may
blank while the mode changes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/plymouth/themes/hyprdebian/hyprdebian.plymouth
The theme, which names the files in
.IR /var/lib/hd-plymouth/theme .
.TP
.I /var/lib/hd-plymouth/theme/
The files that
.BR hd-plymouth-render (8)
renders: the script, the input field in each state, the dot and
.IR background.png ,
a link to the background.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hd-plymouth/fonts
The font files that the hook puts in the initramfs.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hd-plymouth/templates/hyprdebian.script.j2
The template of the script.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hd-plymouth/plymouthd.conf
The config of plymouthd that the package copies to
.IR /etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf .
.TP
.I /etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf.plymouth
The config of plymouth, diverted.
.TP
.I /usr/share/initramfs-tools/hooks/hd-plymouth
Renders the splash and puts it and its fonts in the initramfs.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hd-plymouth-handoff.service
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/plymouth-quit-wait.service.d/hd-desktop.conf
.TQ
.I /usr/libexec/hd-desktop/hd-plymouth-handoff
Hand the splash over to Hyprland.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/hd-desktop/copyright
The copyright and license of the theme.
